{
  "dom_id": "step-q214208",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Find the solutions of $92x≡24\\,(\\text{mod}\\,284).$ Express your answers as nonnegative integers smaller than $284,$ and in ascending order. \n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that $\\text{gcd}(92,284)=4.$*\n\n${x}_{1}≡$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≡$\n{{field-2}}\n$,\\,{x}_{3}≡$\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,{x}_{4}≡$\n{{field-4}}\n$\\,(\\text{mod}\\,284)$",
  "worked_solution": "In the given congruence, we have $a=92$ and $n=284$, and we're given that\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(92,284)=4.\n$$\n\nSince $b=24$ is divisible by $4,$ our congruence has $4$ solutions.\n\nNext, we use the division property of congruences:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}92x & ≡24 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,284) \\\\ 4\\cdot 23x & ≡4\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,4\\cdot 71) \\\\ \\cancel{4}\\cdot 23x & ≡\\cancel{4}\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,\\cancel{4}\\cdot 71) \\\\ 23x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that in this new congruence, we have ${a}_{1}=23$ and ${n}_{1}=71$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}({a}_{1},{n}_{1})=\\text{gcd}(23,71)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence has a unique solution modulo $71.$\n\nTo find the solution, we must multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,71).$ To find the inverse of $23\\,(\\text{mod}\\,71),$ we apply the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}71 & = & 23\\cdot 3 & + & 2\\, & ⇔ & \\,2 & = & 71-23\\cdot 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 23 & = & 11\\cdot 2 & + & 1\\, & ⇔ & \\,1 & = & 23-11\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  &  &  \\\\ 11 & = & 11\\cdot 1 & + & 0\\, &  & \\, &  & \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, back-substituting in the usual way gives the following:\n\n$$\n1=34\\cdot 23-11\\cdot 71\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $71,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}34\\cdot 23-11\\cdot 71 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71) \\\\ 34\\cdot 23 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse of $23$ modulo $71$ is $34.$\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $34,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}23x & ≡6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71) \\\\ 34\\cdot 23x & ≡34\\cdot 6 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71) \\\\ x & ≡204 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71) \\\\ x & ≡71\\cdot 2+62 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71) \\\\ x & ≡62 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,71)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the solution $x≡62\\,(\\text{mod}\\,71).$\n\nBut our original congruence was modulo $284$ (not modulo $71$). To find all the solutions modulo $284,$ we repeatedly add $71$ to $62$ modulo $284$ until we reach the number $62$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ≡62 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,284) \\\\ {x}_{2} & ≡62+71≡133 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,284) \\\\ {x}_{3} & ≡133+71≡204 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,284) \\\\ {x}_{4} & ≡204+71≡275 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,284) \\\\ {x}_{5} & ≡275+71≡346≡284+62≡{x}_{1} &  & (\\text{mod}\\,284)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1,2,3,4}≡62,\\,133,\\,204,\\,275\\,(\\text{mod}\\,284).\n$$",
